        <div class="qa-box"><span class="qa-label">Quick answer</span><p>To build a business dashboard in Power BI: <strong>(1)</strong> decide which decisions it supports, <strong>(2)</strong> pick five to eight KPIs, <strong>(3)</strong> connect your data in Power BI Desktop, <strong>(4)</strong> clean and model the data, <strong>(5)</strong> write measures for each KPI, <strong>(6)</strong> design a simple, scannable layout, <strong>(7)</strong> publish and share, and <strong>(8)</strong> schedule refreshes and review it monthly.</p></div>
        <p class="updated-note">Last updated October 2026 · Written for US small and mid-sized businesses</p>

        <p>A good <strong>business dashboard</strong> answers one question in about five seconds: are we on track, and where should I look first? A bad one is a wall of charts that nobody opens after the first week. The difference is rarely the software. It comes down to deciding what the dashboard is for before you build it.</p>
        <p>This guide walks through building a practical dashboard in Microsoft Power BI, step by step, using examples from small businesses, e-commerce brands, and SaaS teams. If you want background first, read our explainer on <a href="what-is-business-intelligence-guide.html">what business intelligence is</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="what-is">What is a business dashboard?</h2>
        <p>A business dashboard is a single screen that displays your most important metrics, usually with charts, numbers, and filters, drawn from live data. It replaces manual reports with a view that refreshes automatically. Dashboards generally fall into three types:</p>
        <div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
          <tr><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Example metrics</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Operational</td><td>Monitor day-to-day activity</td><td>Orders today, open support tickets, stock levels</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Analytical</td><td>Explore trends and causes</td><td>Revenue by channel, cohort retention, margin by product</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Strategic</td><td>Track progress against goals</td><td>Revenue vs. target, customer acquisition cost, churn</td></tr>
        </table></div>
        <p>Most small businesses should start with one strategic page and one operational page, then add analytical detail where questions keep coming up.</p>

        <h2 id="step-1">Step 1: Start with decisions, not data</h2>
        <p>Write down the three to five decisions someone makes every week. Examples: how much to spend on ads, whether to reorder stock, which leads to follow up first, whether churn is rising. Every chart on your dashboard should help with one of those decisions. If a chart does not, leave it out.</p>

        <h2 id="step-2">Step 2: Choose five to eight KPIs</h2>
        <p>A KPI (key performance indicator) is a number that tells you whether you are on track. Pick a small set and define each in one sentence. Here are starting points by business type:</p>
        <div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
          <tr><th>Business type</th><th>Useful KPIs</th></tr>
          <tr><td>E-commerce</td><td>Revenue, orders, average order value, conversion rate, ad spend and return on ad spend, repeat purchase rate</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SaaS</td><td>Monthly recurring revenue, churn, trial-to-paid conversion, customer acquisition cost, support ticket volume</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Services / agency</td><td>Pipeline value, win rate, project margin, utilization, invoices outstanding</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Local business</td><td>Bookings, revenue by location, new vs. returning customers, review rating</td></tr>
        </table></div>
        <p>Agree on definitions before building. "Revenue" before or after refunds? "Customer" means anyone who signed up or anyone who paid? Disputes here are the number one reason dashboards lose trust.</p>

        <h2 id="step-3">Step 3: Connect your data in Power BI Desktop</h2>
        <p>Download Power BI Desktop from Microsoft, which is free to use for building reports. Select <code>Get data</code> and choose your source. Power BI supports many connectors, including Excel and CSV files, SQL databases, Google Analytics, Salesforce, and cloud sources, and others can be reached through the connectors Microsoft provides or through third-party tools. Connect directly where possible instead of exporting files by hand, so the dashboard can refresh on its own.</p>
        <p>If your data sits in several systems that do not connect easily, such as an ad platform, a CRM, and a payment processor, expect this step to take the longest. That is normal.</p>

        <h2 id="step-4">Step 4: Clean and model the data</h2>
        <p>Open <code>Transform data</code> to use Power Query. Remove duplicates, fix data types (dates as dates, numbers as numbers), standardize names, and filter out test records. Then organize the tables into a simple model: a central table of facts such as orders, linked to smaller tables for customers, products, and dates. This layout, often called a star schema, makes calculations faster and more reliable.</p>
        <p>Always create a dedicated date table. It lets you compare periods, such as this month against last month, without awkward workarounds.</p>

        <h2 id="step-5">Step 5: Write measures for your KPIs</h2>
        <p>Measures are calculations that Power BI computes on the fly, written in a language called DAX. Start with simple ones:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>Total Revenue = SUM(Sales[Amount])</code></li>
          <li><code>Order Count = COUNTROWS(Sales)</code></li>
          <li><code>Average Order Value = DIVIDE([Total Revenue], [Order Count])</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Then add comparisons, such as revenue for the same period last year. Keep a short document listing each measure and what it means, so the logic is not trapped in one person's head.</p>

        <h2 id="step-6">Step 6: Design a layout people can scan</h2>
        <p>Good dashboard design follows a few principles:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Put the most important number top left.</strong> Readers scan from the top left across.</li>
          <li><strong>Use cards for headline KPIs,</strong> line charts for trends over time, and bar charts for comparisons.</li>
          <li><strong>Limit colors.</strong> Use color to highlight what needs attention, not to decorate.</li>
          <li><strong>Add filters sparingly,</strong> such as date range and location, rather than dozens of slicers.</li>
          <li><strong>Label clearly.</strong> Titles should state what the chart shows and the time period.</li>
          <li><strong>Check it on a phone.</strong> Power BI offers a mobile layout, and many owners check numbers on the go.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid pie charts with many slices, 3D effects, and dual axes unless you have a good reason. Simple wins.</p>

        <h2 id="step-7">Step 7: Publish, share, and refresh</h2>
        <p>Select <code>Publish</code> to send your report to the Power BI service, where colleagues can view it in a browser or on mobile. Set up a scheduled refresh so the data updates automatically, and use a gateway if your data sits on a local computer or private server. Sharing with others usually requires paid licensing, so confirm what your Microsoft plan includes before promising access to the whole team.</p>

        <h2 id="step-8">Step 8: Review and improve monthly</h2>
        <p>The first version is never the last. Each month, check which pages people actually open, ask what question they still cannot answer, and remove clutter. Dashboards that are never revisited slowly drift out of date as the business changes. This is the most common reason projects stall, and it is covered further in our post on <a href="power-bi-consulting-for-small-business.html">Power BI consulting for small business</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="forecasting">Adding simple forecasting</h2>
        <p>Once your history is reliable, you can add <strong>business forecasting tools</strong> to the dashboard. In Power BI, line charts include an analytics option that projects a trend forward with a confidence band. For better results, compare any forecast to a naive baseline, such as the same period last year. If the sophisticated version does not beat the simple one, keep the simple one. Always show the range, not just a single predicted number.</p>

        <h2 id="mistakes">Common dashboard mistakes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Too many visuals on one page.</strong> Split detail onto separate pages.</li>
          <li><strong>Unclear metric definitions.</strong> Write them down and display them in a tooltip or info page.</li>
          <li><strong>Manual data exports.</strong> They break the moment someone forgets.</li>
          <li><strong>No owner.</strong> Assign one person responsible for accuracy.</li>
          <li><strong>Building for yourself, not users.</strong> Show a draft to the people who will use it and watch where they get confused.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="diy-or-hire">Build it yourself or get help?</h2>
        <p>If your data lives in one or two clean sources and you have a few days, the steps above are enough to build a useful first dashboard. If data is spread across many tools, definitions are contested, or the dashboard is important to leadership, working with a consultant usually gets you a reliable result faster. Our guide to <a href="power-bi-consulting-cost.html">Power BI consulting cost</a> explains typical pricing models, so you can budget before you talk to anyone.</p>

        <h2 id="our-approach">How Tycoon Clap builds business dashboards</h2>
        <p>We start from the decisions your team makes, connect your real data sources, define each metric once, and build a small set of dashboards sized to your business. We also hand over documentation and a short training session so your team can run it. If you would like help with yours, book a strategy call and tell us which decision you want better data for.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
          <details open><summary>How do I build a business dashboard in Power BI?</summary><p class="faq-a">Define the decisions the dashboard supports, choose five to eight KPIs, connect your data sources in Power BI Desktop, clean and model the data, write measures for your KPIs, design a simple layout, publish it to the Power BI service, and schedule a data refresh.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is Power BI free for building dashboards?</summary><p class="faq-a">Power BI Desktop is free to download and build in. Sharing dashboards with other people through the Power BI service generally requires a paid license, and the exact options depend on your Microsoft plan, so check current Microsoft licensing before you roll out to a team.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How many KPIs should a business dashboard have?</summary><p class="faq-a">Most effective dashboards focus on roughly five to eight key metrics on the main page, with detail available on additional pages. Fewer, well-chosen metrics are easier to act on than dozens of charts.</p></details>
          <details><summary>How long does it take to build a business dashboard?</summary><p class="faq-a">A simple dashboard on clean data can take a few days. A first dashboard across several systems typically takes a few weeks, because most of the work is connecting and cleaning data, not drawing charts.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can Power BI forecast future sales?</summary><p class="faq-a">Power BI includes a basic forecasting feature for line charts, and you can also build forecasts with measures or connect to other models. Treat any forecast as an estimate, compare it with a simple baseline such as last period's value, and show the uncertainty.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Should I hire a Power BI consultant or build it myself?</summary><p class="faq-a">Building a first dashboard yourself works if your data is in one or two clean sources and you have time to learn. A consultant makes sense when data is spread across tools, definitions are disputed, or you need something dependable quickly. Either way, insist on documentation.</p></details>
        </div>
